1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,627
A potato slice.

2
00:00:01,627 --> 00:00:02,963
A ball of cotton.

3
00:00:02,963 --> 00:00:04,917
One drop of iodine on each.

4
00:00:05,317 --> 00:00:09,533
Potato and cotton are both built
from the same sugar, glucose.

5
00:00:09,983 --> 00:00:11,849
In the lab, Aarav tried it.

6
00:00:11,849 --> 00:00:13,561
The potato turned blue-black.

7
00:00:13,561 --> 00:00:14,866
The cotton did not.

8
00:00:14,866 --> 00:00:18,219
So he asked: if both are
glucose, why the difference?

9
00:00:18,219 --> 00:00:20,982
And how does a chain
of sugar hold a colour?

10
00:00:21,332 --> 00:00:23,097
Here is the short answer.

11
00:00:23,097 --> 00:00:27,637
Potato is full of starch, which coils
into a spring that traps iodine.

12
00:00:27,637 --> 00:00:30,656
Cotton is cellulose,
which has no such coils.

13
00:00:31,156 --> 00:00:34,714
By the end, you will know the main
polysaccharides, the iodine test, and

14
00:00:34,714 --> 00:00:36,564
the building blocks of nucleic acids.

15
00:00:37,214 --> 00:00:39,280
First, what is a polysaccharide?

16
00:00:39,630 --> 00:00:40,834
Poly means many.

17
00:00:40,834 --> 00:00:42,382
Saccharide means sugar.

18
00:00:42,382 --> 00:00:45,408
So a polysaccharide is
a long chain of sugars.

19
00:00:45,858 --> 00:00:47,576
Think of a cotton thread.

20
00:00:47,576 --> 00:00:50,956
Each small sugar unit is a
monosaccharide, one sugar.

21
00:00:50,956 --> 00:00:53,113
Hundreds join to make the thread.

22
00:00:53,113 --> 00:00:57,753
Polysaccharides stay behind in the acid
insoluble fraction, as big molecules.

23
00:00:58,403 --> 00:01:00,772
Let's zoom into a plant to find one.

24
00:01:01,122 --> 00:01:03,865
A plant cell is boxed in by a cell wall.

25
00:01:03,865 --> 00:01:05,944
That wall is made of cellulose.

26
00:01:06,444 --> 00:01:07,392
Zoom closer.

27
00:01:07,392 --> 00:01:12,066
Cellulose is a long, straight chain
of sugar units, lying side by side.

28
00:01:12,566 --> 00:01:13,789
Zoom to one unit.

29
00:01:13,789 --> 00:01:15,988
It is glucose, a ring-shaped sugar.

30
00:01:15,988 --> 00:01:19,127
Cellulose has only glucose,
so it is a homopolymer.

31
00:01:19,777 --> 00:01:21,729
Now meet five
polysaccharides, step by step.

32
00:01:22,079 --> 00:01:22,979
Step one.

33
00:01:22,902 --> 00:01:25,160
Cellulose, made only of glucose.

34
00:01:25,160 --> 00:01:27,017
It forms plant cell walls.

35
00:01:27,017 --> 00:01:29,747
Paper and cotton fibre are cellulose too.

36
00:01:30,497 --> 00:01:31,397
Step two.

37
00:01:31,248 --> 00:01:34,158
Starch, a variant that
stores energy in plants.

38
00:01:34,908 --> 00:01:35,808
Step three.

39
00:01:35,796 --> 00:01:39,000
Glycogen, the animal
version, with many branches.

40
00:01:39,750 --> 00:01:40,650
Step four.

41
00:01:40,598 --> 00:01:43,637
Inulin, a polymer of
fructose, another sugar.

42
00:01:44,387 --> 00:01:45,287
Step five.

43
00:01:45,199 --> 00:01:48,701
Chitin, in the hard outer
skeleton of insects and crabs.

44
00:01:48,701 --> 00:01:52,670
It is built from amino-sugars,
which are chemically changed sugars.

45
00:01:53,420 --> 00:01:54,471
Remember this.

46
00:01:54,471 --> 00:01:55,875
Starch holds iodine.

47
00:01:55,875 --> 00:01:57,073
Cellulose cannot.

48
00:01:57,673 --> 00:01:59,840
Now watch the iodine test happen.

49
00:02:00,190 --> 00:02:02,707
Starch coils into a helix, like a spring.

50
00:02:02,707 --> 00:02:05,713
Iodine molecules slide into
the middle of the spring.

51
00:02:06,163 --> 00:02:08,739
The starch with iodine looks blue.

52
00:02:08,739 --> 00:02:13,847
Cellulose has no complex helices, so
iodine cannot be held, and no blue

53
00:02:13,847 --> 00:02:14,747
appears.

54
00:02:15,288 --> 00:02:17,677
Now the second big family, nucleic acids.

55
00:02:17,677 --> 00:02:19,456
Here are their building blocks.

56
00:02:19,806 --> 00:02:21,945
A nucleotide has three parts.

57
00:02:21,945 --> 00:02:26,221
A nitrogen base, a five-carbon
sugar, and a phosphate group.

58
00:02:26,671 --> 00:02:29,855
A base joined to a sugar is
a nucleoside, like adenosine.

59
00:02:29,855 --> 00:02:33,450
Add a phosphate, and it becomes
a nucleotide, like adenylic acid.

60
00:02:33,450 --> 00:02:37,673
Nucleic acids are long chains of nucleotides,
so they are called polynucleotides.

61
00:02:38,323 --> 00:02:40,218
The bases come in two shapes.

62
00:02:40,568 --> 00:02:43,372
Adenine and guanine are
purines, with two rings.

63
00:02:43,372 --> 00:02:46,836
Cytosine, uracil and thymine
are pyrimidines, with one ring.

64
00:02:47,286 --> 00:02:48,994
The sugar decides the name.

65
00:02:48,994 --> 00:02:52,069
With deoxyribose, it is
deoxyribonucleic acid, DNA.

66
00:02:52,069 --> 00:02:54,694
With ribose, it is ribonucleic acid, RNA.

67
00:02:55,294 --> 00:02:56,194
Your turn.

68
00:02:56,092 --> 00:02:58,356
A nucleic acid has the sugar ribose.

69
00:02:58,356 --> 00:02:59,777
Is it DNA, or RNA?

70
00:02:59,777 --> 00:03:00,951
Pause and decide.

71
00:03:04,151 --> 00:03:05,051
RNA!

72
00:03:04,540 --> 00:03:06,335
Ribose sugar makes ribonucleic acid.

73
00:03:06,335 --> 00:03:07,427
DNA has deoxyribose.

74
00:03:08,077 --> 00:03:09,709
Now, a worked example.

75
00:03:09,709 --> 00:03:12,636
Name the big molecule
from its building block.

76
00:03:12,986 --> 00:03:15,394
Chain one is made of glucose units only.

77
00:03:15,394 --> 00:03:18,551
That is cellulose, starch
or glycogen, a homopolymer.

78
00:03:19,001 --> 00:03:21,383
Chain two is made of fructose units.

79
00:03:21,383 --> 00:03:22,521
That is inulin.

80
00:03:22,971 --> 00:03:27,209
Chain three is made of units with
a base, a sugar and a phosphate.

81
00:03:27,209 --> 00:03:28,814
That is a nucleic acid.

82
00:03:29,464 --> 00:03:30,925
Now, the exam trap.

83
00:03:31,275 --> 00:03:34,124
Many students think nucleosides are
the building blocks of nucleic acids.

84
00:03:34,524 --> 00:03:37,263
But a nucleoside is
only a base and a sugar.

85
00:03:37,263 --> 00:03:41,224
Nucleic acids are made of nucleotides,
which also carry a phosphate.

86
00:03:41,874 --> 00:03:43,060
Where is this asked?

87
00:03:43,060 --> 00:03:46,685
As assertion-reason or statement
questions on starch and iodine, cellulose,

88
00:03:46,685 --> 00:03:49,082
inulin, chitin, and
purines and pyrimidines.

89
00:03:49,682 --> 00:03:50,858
Exam level now.

90
00:03:50,858 --> 00:03:52,703
An assertion and a reason.

91
00:03:52,703 --> 00:03:53,929
Pause and solve.

92
00:03:59,329 --> 00:04:00,393
The answer is A.

93
00:04:00,393 --> 00:04:03,313
Both are true, and the reason
explains the assertion.

94
00:04:03,813 --> 00:04:07,223
Starch forms helices, and
the helix holds iodine.

95
00:04:07,723 --> 00:04:09,889
The starch with iodine is blue.

96
00:04:09,889 --> 00:04:13,180
Cellulose has no helices,
so it stays uncoloured.

97
00:04:13,780 --> 00:04:15,320
Here is your recap.

98
00:04:15,320 --> 00:04:16,903
Screenshot this card.

99
00:04:20,103 --> 00:04:22,289
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

100
00:04:22,689 --> 00:04:23,589
One.

101
00:04:23,147 --> 00:04:25,826
Why does cellulose not
turn blue with iodine?

102
00:04:26,726 --> 00:04:27,626
Two.

103
00:04:27,136 --> 00:04:29,370
What are the three parts of a nucleotide?

104
00:04:30,270 --> 00:04:31,170
Three.

105
00:04:30,662 --> 00:04:31,650
Name the two purines.

106
00:04:32,750 --> 00:04:36,147
Next time: how does a straight chain
of amino acids fold into a working

107
00:04:36,147 --> 00:04:37,047
machine?

108
00:04:36,678 --> 00:04:38,757
We find out in the structure of proteins.
